// tests/cpu-limit.py (`%{cpu_limit}`), the CPU-time ceiling of the size runs
// of the long suite, in units of the `version` run of the same program, on
// both sides. The program here (Inputs/burn.py) burns its argument in
// seconds of CPU; its `version` only starts. A run that burns 0.01 s passes a
// ceiling of 4 (about 1.9 alone, and a busy machine only lowers it: the `version`
// run grows more than the burn); a run that burns 0.2 s fails it
// (cpu-limit-slow.test: it is measured three times, so it burns 0.6 s in all,
// a test of its own to stay under the 2 s limit of the normal suite); the
// exit status must be the expected one.
// RUN: printf '#!/bin/sh\nexec %python %S/Inputs/burn.py "$@"\n' > %t.burn
// RUN: chmod +x %t.burn
// RUN: %{cpu_limit} 4 4 -- %t.burn 0.01
// RUN: %{cpu_limit} 4 4 --status 3 -- %t.burn 0 3
// RUN: not %{cpu_limit} 4 4 -- %t.burn 0 3 2>&1 | FileCheck %s --check-prefix=STATUS
// STATUS: cpu-limit: exit status 3, expected 0
